Drake's failure form is data: gendrake.RefusalOf returns the codec refusals as Problems, and the emitter raises the first (GitHub #11)

OWNING ISSUE: GitHub #11 (open; stays open). Nothing on the tracker was touched.

WHY. After 0b53e39 what was still hand-written on the emit side was refusal alone: the three codec guards in `Drake.emit`
and the element refusals. They had stayed because drake has no way for a definition to raise. Draco already has a way to
REPORT: `draco.Problem` (a subject and a message), which the validation rules produce. Dev accepted "Accepted, build it" on
the recommendation that the failure form be data — a definition returns the problems, the emitter raises when there are
any — starting with the three codec guards, the element refusals to follow as points of their own.

WHAT.
- New trio `gendrake.RefusalOf`: factory `par definition TypeDefinition`, one element `problems [Problem]`. It reports, in
  the order the emitter judged them, a codec discriminator (drake has no syntax for one), a duplicate payload field and a
  payload field that is not a name, each one Problem whose subject is the definition's name and whose message is the
  emitter's, unchanged. Authored in drake with its `uses` record, JSON `DrakeCLI parse`, Scala `GeneratorCLI generate`;
  listed by GenDrake, whose trio moves with it. Both generated files stable on the recompiled engine.
- Drake.scala: `emit` reads `RefusalOf(td).problems` and raises the first message; the three hand-written guards are
  removed. The parser's own two payload checks (`Drake.parse: …`) are untouched.
- RefusalOfTest (6), the 92nd suite: nothing to refuse; each refusal with its message and subject; all three reported in
  order; the definition read once and lazily; the live emitter raising the first; no definition of the corpus refused.

ONE DIFFERENCE. The two payload guards were `require`s, so their message carried the host prefix "requirement failed: ";
the emitter now raises every refusal as an IllegalArgumentException with the Problem's message alone. The discriminator
refusal was a RuntimeException and still is one (by subclass). No existing test changed.

PROBED IN BUILDING. The pattern match on a text (`field.matches("…")`), unprobed at the recommendation, is carried as a
call tree and generates as written. ONE SHAPE CHANGE the parser forced: a lambda binder named `message` is read as the
actor section's keyword ("a lambda has no arrow"), so the binder is `reason` (DRACO.md §6).

NOT BUILT, not accepted: the element refusals (`refuseDefinition`, `refuseElement`, `refuseUnrenderable`, `bareKeyword`)
as Problems; they stay hand-written in the emitter.

